# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
common_form	one
excess	one
alike	one
equal	one
# There is a possible strain in common_form. S3's example (air and fire sharing heat) treats a "common form" as a quality that several things participate in.
# S4 speaks instead of "the same nature" being in both Father and Son, which suggests one identical nature rather than a shared quality.
# I read P4 as applying S3's general principle to a case, not as a change of sense, since the text explicitly equates the two.
# The same applies to "participates" in excess.
